1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Coach: An experience may belong to a team

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Coach: while particular actions belong to different people.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,428
Coach: A clear account makes your own part visible

4
00:00:09,428 --> 00:00:12,000
Coach: without taking credit for everyone’s work.

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,857
Coach: Neither I nor we is automatically the right choice.

6
00:00:16,207 --> 00:00:17,921
Coach: In our fictional example,

7
00:00:17,921 --> 00:00:23,064
Coach: three volunteers prepared a one-page guide for a community open afternoon.

8
00:00:23,064 --> 00:00:26,921
Coach: The participant drew the map, Maya listed session times,

9
00:00:26,921 --> 00:00:29,064
Coach: and Noah assembled the guide.

10
00:00:29,064 --> 00:00:32,921
Coach: These role facts stay the same throughout the examples.

11
00:00:33,271 --> 00:00:35,414
Coach: The day before the event,

12
00:00:35,414 --> 00:00:41,414
Coach: the participant noticed Studio B on the map and Blue Room on the schedule.

13
00:00:41,414 --> 00:00:46,128
Coach: They asked the coordinator whether the names meant the same room,

14
00:00:46,128 --> 00:00:49,128
Coach: rather than selecting a name by guessing.

15
00:00:49,478 --> 00:00:56,335
Coach: After confirmation that both names meant the same room and its door sign said Blue Room,

16
00:00:56,335 --> 00:01:00,621
Coach: the participant changed the map label and told the others.

17
00:01:00,621 --> 00:01:02,335
Coach: Maya checked the schedule.

18
00:01:02,335 --> 00:01:04,478
Coach: Noah assembled the updated guide.

19
00:01:04,478 --> 00:01:09,192
Coach: An individual action and a team result can be described together.

20
00:01:09,542 --> 00:01:12,970
Coach: We prepared a guide describes the shared task.

21
00:01:12,970 --> 00:01:16,827
Coach: I changed the map label describes the participant’s action.

22
00:01:16,827 --> 00:01:20,685
Coach: The distinction is not about making one sound better;

23
00:01:20,685 --> 00:01:24,113
Coach: it helps the listener understand who did what.

24
00:01:24,463 --> 00:01:29,177
Coach: Privately separate one personal action from one contribution by someone else.

25
00:01:29,177 --> 00:01:31,320
Coach: Use the supplied facts rather

26
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:34,749
Coach: than filling a gap with an impressive responsibility.

27
00:01:34,749 --> 00:01:39,892
Coach: Nothing about the case establishes that the participant led the whole project.

28
00:01:48,242 --> 00:01:52,956
Maya: What was your own part in preparing and updating the guide?

29
00:01:53,306 --> 00:01:56,735
Coach: Compare three possible accounts of the same case.

30
00:01:56,735 --> 00:02:00,592
Coach: Which answers Maya’s question while keeping everyone’s contribution accurate?

31
00:02:00,592 --> 00:02:02,735
Coach: Listen for a personal action,

32
00:02:02,735 --> 00:02:08,306
Coach: and notice whether the words also leave room for the other volunteers’ work.

33
00:02:08,656 --> 00:02:12,513
Coach: One alternative deliberately claims more than the facts support.

34
00:02:12,513 --> 00:02:16,799
Coach: Read it as wording to examine, not an added event.

35
00:02:16,799 --> 00:02:20,656
Coach: The source facts, not the confidence of a reply,

36
00:02:20,656 --> 00:02:23,227
Coach: decide whether its attribution is accurate.

37
00:02:31,577 --> 00:02:36,291
You: I drew the map, checked the conflicting names with the coordinator,

38
00:02:36,291 --> 00:02:39,292
You: and changed its label to Blue Room.

39
00:02:39,292 --> 00:02:41,863
You: I then told the other volunteers.

40
00:02:42,213 --> 00:02:44,356
Maya: That identifies the map work.

41
00:02:44,356 --> 00:02:49,070
Maya: How did it connect with the schedule and the assembled guide?

42
00:02:49,420 --> 00:02:52,849
Coach: This account names actions supported by the case

43
00:02:52,849 --> 00:02:55,420
Coach: without taking over the other roles.

44
00:02:55,420 --> 00:02:59,706
Coach: The surrounding explanation can add that Maya checked the schedule

45
00:02:59,706 --> 00:03:02,277
Coach: and Noah assembled the updated guide.

46
00:03:02,277 --> 00:03:05,277
Coach: Specific ownership does not require erasing collaboration.

47
00:03:05,627 --> 00:03:09,913
Coach: The participant handled the map and checked its conflicting label;

48
00:03:09,913 --> 00:03:13,341
Coach: Maya handled the schedule; Noah assembled the guide.

49
00:03:13,341 --> 00:03:18,912
Coach: Keep that division clear when you put the account into your own words.

50
00:03:18,912 --> 00:03:22,341
Coach: A shared result can include several distinct contributions.

51
00:03:22,691 --> 00:03:26,977
Coach: Say enough about the others to make the work understandable,

52
00:03:26,977 --> 00:03:34,691
Coach: but do not let a long team description hide your answer to a question about your own part.

53
00:03:34,691 --> 00:03:38,120
Coach: The right balance depends on what was asked.

54
00:03:38,470 --> 00:03:40,613
Coach: Privately explain the participant’s role,

55
00:03:40,613 --> 00:03:44,041
Coach: or use a truthful experience of your own.

56
00:03:44,041 --> 00:03:47,041
Coach: Where does we describe the team accurately,

57
00:03:47,041 --> 00:03:50,898
Coach: and where would I make your own action clearer?

58
00:03:50,898 --> 00:03:56,041
Coach: Try changing one sentence while keeping the ownership of every action intact.

59
00:04:04,391 --> 00:04:09,105
Coach: If you formed a version, check who each action belongs to.

60
00:04:09,105 --> 00:04:13,820
Coach: Do not borrow a responsibility to make the answer sound stronger.

61
00:04:13,820 --> 00:04:19,391
Coach: Next, we will explain why the participant chose to check the room name.
